{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-symmetric-point-channel-collapse","@type":["folio-assistant-core:Proposition","doco:Section"],"label":"prop:symmetric-point-channel-collapse","kind":"proposition","title":"Channel collapse at the symmetric point","tags":["symmetric-point","hecke","degeneration"],"leanRef":"qou:QOU.symmetric_point_channel_collapse","text":"symmetric-point-channel-collapse.md","leanSource":"symmetric-point-channel-collapse.lean","provenance":"authored","html":"<p><a id=\"prop:symmetric-point-channel-collapse\"></a>\n<strong>Proposition.</strong> <em>Channel collapse at the symmetric point</em></p>\n<p>At the symmetric point <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q = 1</code>, the three Hecke channels <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\sigma</code>, <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\sigma^{-1}</code>, and <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(\\sigma + \\sigma^{-1})/2</code> degenerate to a single operator. The reason is a purely algebraic identity in any field.</p>\n<p><strong>Proposition.</strong> <em>Let <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">R</code> be a field with <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">2 \\neq 0</code> and let <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">s \\in R</code> satisfy <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">s^2 = 1</code>. Then <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">s^{-1} = s</code>, and consequently</em></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-math math-display\">  \\frac{s + s^{-1}}{2} \\;=\\; s .</code></pre>\n"}
